---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Frontier Research — monogate.org"
  description="Active Monogate research fronts: EML Advantage Lab, guarded lowering, proof-carrying rescue packets, MachLib witnesses, and bounded public claims."
>
  <section class="frontier-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Monogate Research</p>
    <h1>Frontier map</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The current frontier is no longer "EML wins everywhere." It is a
      repeatable review loop: EML expressions become packets, packets become
      guard decisions, guard decisions become lowering plans or proof
      obligations, and monogate.org keeps the public claim boundary honest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Current center</p>
    <h2>EML Advantage Lab</h2>
    <p>
      The Advantage Lab asks the corrected research question: where does EML
      help, where does standard/protected math win, and which claims remain
      blocked? The first scoreboard is deliberately mixed: EML is strongest as
      a generator, proof/search grammar, and teaching lens; standard or
      protected math currently wins many runtime and stability cases.
    </p>
    <ul class="metrics">
      <li><strong>1</strong><span>EML win</span></li>
      <li><strong>4</strong><span>standard wins</span></li>
      <li><strong>3</strong><span>mixed cases</span></li>
      <li><strong>1</strong><span>research-only case</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="metrics-asof">
      Scoreboard frozen at 2026-04-24 (first Advantage Lab fixture).
      The live counts are at <a href="https://monogate.dev/explorer/eml-advantage">monogate.dev/explorer/eml-advantage</a>;
      they may have moved since.
    </p>
    <div class="links">
      <a href="https://monogate.dev/explorer/eml-advantage">Open Advantage Lab</a>
      <a href="/blog/eml-advantage-lab">Read research note</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Active instrument</p>
    <h2>Proof-carrying rescue suite</h2>
    <p>
      The rescue suite tracks four optimizer boundary events through named
      rescue operators and replayable witness packets. The generated Explorer
      fixture is derived from the Forge manifest, replay JSON, obligation
      registry, and approval gate.
    </p>
    <div class="links">
      <a href="https://monogate.dev/explorer/rescue-suite">Open Explorer</a>
      <a href="/blog/proof-carrying-rescue-suite-v0">Read v0 note</a>
      <a href="/blog/how-to-read-the-rescue-suite">Read the guide</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="grid" aria-label="Frontier research fronts">
    <article>
      <p class="tag">compiler boundary</p>
      <h2>Guarded lowering</h2>
      <p>
        A9 through A11.3 convert EML advantage evidence into conservative
        compiler policy: preserve EML for proof/search shape, lower near-zero
        `exp(x)-1` to protected `expm1`, lower softplus/log-sum-exp to
        protected forms, require positive-domain guards, and block unstable
        deep trees.
      </p>
      <a href="https://monogate.dev/explorer/eml-packets/builder">Open packet builder</a>
    </article>

    <article>
      <p class="tag">symbolic search</p>
      <h2>PySR reality check</h2>
      <p>
        The first real private PySR run did not show a robust EML grammar
        advantage on the prime-residual fixture. That is useful evidence: it
        narrows the claim to better feature design, sharper targets, and
        pre-registered controls instead of treating EML universality as a
        performance claim.
      </p>
      <a href="https://monogate.dev/explorer/eml-symbolic-regression">Open template search</a>
    </article>

    <article>
      <p class="tag">machlib</p>
      <h2>Small checked witnesses</h2>
      <p>
        MachLib has small witnesses for softplus positivity, sigmoid
        denominator nonzero, <code>eml(x, 1) = exp(x)</code>, and the
        Atlas-facing subtraction boundary
        <code>eml(log(v), exp(u)) = v - u</code> under <code>0 &lt; v</code>.
        These are scoped footholds, not full EML language semantics.
      </p>
      <a href="/blog/proof-carrying-rescue-status">See status table</a>
    </article>

    <article>
      <p class="tag">claim boundary</p>
      <h2>What is claimed</h2>
      <p>
        We claim generated analysis artifacts, simulated traces, replay checks,
        and explicit proof-obligation names. We do not yet claim production
        optimizer rewrites, broad EML superiority, compiler correctness,
        hardware observations, zeta-zero discovery, or fully discharged formal
        proofs for the whole stack.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Surface split</p>
    <h2>Research record, workbench, visualization</h2>
    <p>
      monogate.org is the research record. monogate.dev is the developer
      playground where people inspect packets and run tools. 1op.io is the
      games and visualization surface. This split matters: the dev site should
      be usable, while the research site carries the claims, caveats, and
      public status.
    </p>
    <div class="links">
      <a href="https://monogate.dev">Open dev playground</a>
      <a href="https://1op.io">Open 1op.io</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .frontier-hero {
      padding: 36px 0 20px;
    }
    .eyebrow,
    .tag {
      margin: 0 0 8px;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--orange);
    }
    h1 {
      margin: 0 0 14px;
      font-size: 2.2rem;
      line-height: 1.1;
    }
    h2 {
      margin: 0 0 10px;
      font-size: 1.12rem;
      line-height: 1.25;
    }
    .lede,
    p {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.7;
    }
    .lede {
      max-width: 720px;
      font-size: 1.02rem;
    }
    .panel,
    article {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 8px;
      background: var(--surface);
      padding: 20px;
    }
    .grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 14px;
      margin: 18px 0;
    }
    .links {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 8px;
      margin-top: 14px;
    }
    .metrics {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 10px;
      margin: 18px 0 4px;
      padding: 0;
      list-style: none;
    }
    .metrics li {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.015);
    }
    .metrics strong {
      display: block;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      color: var(--orange);
      font-size: 1.4rem;
      line-height: 1;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }
    .metrics-asof {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.55;
      margin: 6px 0 12px;
    }
    .metrics-asof a { color: var(--cyan); }
    .metrics span {
      display: block;
      color: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      line-height: 1.45;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    }
    .links a,
    article a {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.82rem;
    }
    .links a {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
      padding: 7px 10px;
      text-decoration: none;
    }
    code {
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
      .metrics {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      }
      h1 {
        font-size: 1.7rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Base>
